<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Paris (1783)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1783">1783</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Paris of 1783 ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> recognised the independence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and accepted borders reaching the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi River</a> in the west, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> in the north and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> in the south. Separate treaties signed at the same time returned Florida to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and made smaller exchanges with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1783.jpg" alt="Treaty of Paris (1783)" width="320" height="477" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: iDK · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Treaty_of_Paris_(1783).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Peace negotiations began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in April 1782, after the fall of Frederick North's government on 20 March following the British defeat at Yorktown. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> was represented by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-franklin">Benjamin Franklin</a>, John Jay, Henry Laurens and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-adams">John Adams</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> by David Hartley and Richard Oswald. In September 1782 the French foreign minister Vergennes proposed a settlement that would confine independence to the area east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appalachian-mountains">Appalachian Mountains</a>, which the American delegation rejected.</p><p>John Jay told the British he was willing to negotiate directly with them and bypass <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and Prime Minister Lord Shelburne agreed. He saw a chance to split the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> from France and to make the new nation a valuable economic partner. The treaty was drafted on 30 November 1782.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty was signed on 3 September 1783 at the Hôtel d'York in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> by Adams, Franklin, Jay and Hartley. Britain acknowledged the thirteen former colonies, listed by name, as free, sovereign and independent states. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> gained all the land east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi River</a>, north of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> and south of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, and fishing rights on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-banks-of-newfoundland">Grand Banks</a> and in the Gulf of Saint Lawrence. Creditors on either side were to meet no lawful impediment to recovering debts, Congress was to recommend that the states restore confiscated Loyalist property, and prisoners of war were to be released.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> signed separate agreements with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and, provisionally, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. East and West Florida went to Spain without a clear northern boundary, and Spain also received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menorca">Menorca</a>. The Congress of the Confederation ratified the treaty at Annapolis on 14 January 1784, and the ratified copies were exchanged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 12 May 1784.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Historians have often called the treaty very generous to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and some argue that the British aimed at close economic ties and profitable trade. Much of it did not work as written. The states ignored the recommendation to restore Loyalist property, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> kept laws against paying debts to British creditors. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> used its control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> to block American use of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, and a dispute over the Florida boundary lasted until the Treaty of Madrid in 1795.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> kept eight forts on what was technically American soil, citing American failure to compensate Loyalists. They were given up peacefully under the Jay Treaty of 1794. Only Article 1, acknowledging the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> as free, sovereign and independent, remains in force.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Paris (1783)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1779 the Sullivan Expedition destroyed some forty Haudenosaunee towns and their crops, and the <b>Treaty of Paris</b> of 1783 made no provision for Britain’s Native allies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Iroquois Confederacy · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>The loss of the thirteen American colonies, recognised in the <b>Treaty of Paris</b> in 1783, was a severe blow, but trade with the United States recovered quickly and the empire shifted its weight towards India, the Caribbean and the Pacific.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Treaty of Paris</b> of 1783 recognised independence, with a western border on the Mississippi, debts and unrest such as Shays' Rebellion of 1786–1787 persuaded leaders to call the Constitutional Convention at Philadelphia in 1787.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Meeting at the Maryland State House in Annapolis, the Confederation Congress proclaimed its ratification of the <b>Treaty of Paris</b>, the official end of the American Revolutionary War.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-14">14 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>British and American envoys signed the <b>Treaty of Paris</b>, ending the Revolutionary War and fixing the new nation's borders at the Mississippi.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-3">3 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of Paris</b> of 1783 set the border at the 'northwesternmost head of the Connecticut River', which could be read as several different streams.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-indian-stream">Republic of Indian Stream</a></li></ul></section>
